by score descending\\n        candidates_sorted = sorted(candidates, key=lambda idx: scores[idx], reverse=True)\\n        # number to pick from candidates\\n        needed = target - len(forced_set)\\n        selected_candidates = set(candidates_sorted[:needed])\\n        # combine with forced\\n        retained_set = forced_set.union(selected_candidates)\\n        # sort to preserve original order\\n        retained_mask = sorted(retained_set)\\n        history.append(retained_mask)\\n        active = retained_mask  # for next round\\n    retained_indices = active  # final active\\n    return {\\n        \\\"history\\\": history,\\n        \\\"retained_indices\\\": retained_indices\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write solve(case) that processes the given payload. The payload contains round_scores (list of lists of floats), round_targets (list of integers, number of tokens to keep per round?), forced (list of indices that must be kept), and _case_nonce (ignore). Based on the description: \"iterative token compression\" - at each round, rank active indices by descending score, insert forced indices, then fill remaining positions from top scores, preserving original order. Return history (per-round masks?) and retained_indices (final indices). The output schema: history is list of lists of integers (each inner list is a mask of indices retained at that round?), retained_indices is list of integers (final retained indices). We need to implement this.\n\nInterpretation: We have N tokens total. Initially all indices 0..N-1 are active. For each round (i from 0 to len(round_scores)-1): We have score vector for that round (list of floats for each token? But round_scores is list of lists, each inner list length = number of tokens? Actually round_scores[i] is a list of scores for that round, presumably for each token index. But note: In the payload, round_scores[0] length is 8, round_scores[1] length is 8. The round_targets are [6,4] meaning first round keep 6 